{"seconds": 77.5, "exit": 0, "final": "Perfect! After Nf4, the rook and h3 pawn are both undefended. White can only save one. Black is winning (up material or winning the rook).\n\nMain line: **1...Nh5 2.Re2 Rh1+ 3.Kxh1 Nf4** winning the rook or h-pawn.\n\nThe motif is **fork** (Nf4 attacks two pieces).\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Nh5\", \"line\": [\"Nh5\", \"Re2\", \"Rh1+\", \"Kxh1\", \"Nf4\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\"]}\n```\n\n**Coaching note (78 words):**\n\"Look for quiet moves that set up overwhelming tactics. Here, Nh5 prepares a devastating knight fork on f4. If White moves the rook defensively with Re2, you have Rh1+ forcing the king to capture, then Nf4 wins the undefended rook and h-pawn. White's exposed king on h2 is the key weakness. The lesson: sometimes the best move isn't the most forcing one\u2014it's the quiet setup that your opponent can't adequately defend against.\"", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "8/5k2/8/2N1p1p1/1P2Pn1p/2P2P1P/5RPK/1r6 b - - 5 37"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "8/5k2/8/2N1p1p1/1P2Pn1p/2P2P1P/5RPK/1r6 b - - 5 37"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "8/5k2/8/2N1p1p1/1P2Pn1p/2P2P1P/5RPK/1r6 b - - 5 37"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/5k2/8/2N1p1p1/1P2Pn1p/2P2P1P/5RPK/1r6 b - - 5 37", "move": "Rh1"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/5k2/8/2N1p1p1/1P2Pn1p/2P2P1P/5RPK/1r6 b - - 5 37", "move": "Nh5"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-legal_moves", "args": {"fen": "8/5k2/8/2N1p1pn/1P2P2p/2P2P1P/5RPK/1r6 w - - 6 38"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/5k2/8/2N1p1pn/1P2P2p/2P2P1P/5RPK/1r6 w - - 6 38", "move": "Re2"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/5k2/8/2N1p1pn/1P2P2p/2P2P1P/5RPK/1r6 w - - 6 38", "move": "Rf1"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-inspect_move", "args": {"fen": "8/5k2/8/2N1p1pn/1P2P2p/2P2P1P/4R1PK/1r6 b - - 7 38", "move": "Rh1"}}, {"tool